{
  "name": "memphis-postmodern-ui-light",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Memphis/Postmodern design system - a playful 1980s Ettore-Sottsass-inspired aesthetic built on a scattered confetti field of outlined geometric shapes (squiggles, zigzags, dot clusters, arcs), thick black outlines on every colored shape, a simultaneous multi-hue clash palette (hot pink/sunshine yellow/turquoise/cobalt/coral), and rounded friendly typography - for integrating this joyful, toy-like style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, badges), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Memphis / Postmodern\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to any particular industry; the playful 1980s Memphis Group aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: Design as confetti — a joyful clash of bold color, playful geometric confetti-shapes, and thick black outlines, inspired by the Memphis Group (Ettore Sottsass, Milan, early 1980s) and the postmodern rejection of \"good taste\" minimalism. Where modernism said \"less is more,\" Memphis says \"more is more, and it should make you smile.\"\n\n**Vibe**: Playful, energetic, toy-like, celebratory, irreverent, confidently \"wrong.\" Think a birthday party invitation, a Saved by the Bell title sequence, a children's museum — never punk or hostile (that's Neo-brutalism), never elegant restraint (that's Swiss or Bauhaus).\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"Clash on purpose.\" Memphis deliberately combines colors and shapes that conventional taste says shouldn't work together (hot pink next to turquoise next to sunshine yellow), and makes them work through confident, consistent outlining and generous scale. Every shape gets a thick black outline, like a coloring-book page — this is what keeps five clashing colors from reading as chaos.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Neo-brutalism**: Neo-brutalism uses 3 disciplined colors (cream/red/yellow/violet, used sparingly) and reads as punk/raw; Memphis uses 4-5 colors simultaneously and reads as playful/decorative, with confetti shapes as pure ornament rather than structural elements.\n- vs. **Claymorphism**: Claymorphism is soft, puffy, and shadow-driven (3D); Memphis is flat, outlined, and shape-driven (2D, cutout/collage feel).\n- vs. **Bauhaus**: Bauhaus uses a disciplined red/yellow/blue triad in rational compositions; Memphis uses a wider, louder palette in intentionally asymmetric, \"confetti-scattered\" compositions.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nUse these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme. Unlike most systems in this collection, Memphis intentionally uses **several accent hues simultaneously** rather than one disciplined accent.\n*   `background`: `#FFFDF7` (Cream Paper)\n*   `foreground`: `#111111` (Outline Black) — used for all shape outlines and primary text\n*   `muted-foreground`: `#5C5C52`\n*   `accent-pink` (Hot Pink): `#FF5DA2`\n*   `accent-yellow` (Sunshine): `#FFD400`\n*   `accent-teal` (Turquoise): `#2EC4B6`\n*   `accent-blue` (Cobalt): `#3A86FF`\n*   `accent-coral`: `#FF6B6B`\n*   `border`: `#111111` (thick, solid, always black)\n\n**Rule**: use at least 3 of the 5 accent hues together on any given screen — a single-accent Memphis page loses the \"clash\" that defines the style. Balance the loudness with generous cream negative space between shapes.\n\n### Typography\n*   **Family**: A rounded, friendly geometric sans — **'Poppins'** or **'Baloo 2'** (Google Fonts), bold weights (700-800) for headings, regular-to-medium for body.\n*   **Treatment**: Headlines can mix colors word-by-word (each word or line in a different accent hue) — a signature Memphis typographic move. Squiggle underlines (a simple SVG wavy line) replace plain straight underlines for emphasis and links.\n\n### Radius & Shapes\n*   **Cards/containers**: `rounded-2xl` to `rounded-3xl` — soft enough to feel toy-like, not sharp.\n*   **Confetti shapes**: squiggles, zigzags, dot-grids, semicircle arcs, and simple triangles/rectangles — always with a thick black outline, scattered as background decoration at varying scale and rotation.\n\n### The Confetti Field & Outline Rule — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC\n*   **Confetti Field**: 6-12 small decorative shapes (squiggle, zigzag, dot cluster, arc, triangle) scattered across a section's background at low z-index, in the accent palette, each outlined in black. They should feel \"thrown,\" not gridded — vary size, rotation, and spacing.\n*   **Outline Everything**: every colored shape (cards, badges, icon containers, confetti) gets a `2-3px solid` black outline — this is what makes clashing colors read as a coherent, confident system instead of noise.\n*   **Shape-as-decoration bleeds off edges**: confetti shapes can be partially cropped by their container's edge, reinforcing the \"scattered pieces\" feel.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Shape**: `rounded-full` or `rounded-2xl`, filled with one accent color, `border-2 border-foreground`.\n*   **Interaction**: slight rotation on hover (`hover:rotate-2` or `hover:-rotate-1`) plus a small lift — playful, not mechanical (that's Neo-brutalism's press-down).\n*   **Typography**: bold, friendly, sentence case (not aggressive all-caps).\n\n### Cards / Containers\n*   **Structure**: `background` (cream) fill, `border-2 border-foreground`, `rounded-2xl` to `rounded-3xl`.\n*   **Decoration**: one confetti shape (a dot cluster or squiggle) placed at a corner, partially overlapping the card edge.\n*   **Hover**: slight rotation + lift, consistent with buttons.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `rounded-full` or `rounded-xl`, `border-2 border-foreground`, `bg-white`.\n*   **Focus**: border color shifts to an accent hue (e.g. `accent-blue`), with a small squiggle-underline appearing beneath.\n\n### Badges\n*   **Shape**: pill or shape-clipped chip, one accent fill, black outline, often with a small confetti dot beside the label.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Container Widths**: `max-w-7xl` for marketing, generous `gap-8` to `gap-12` between elements.\n*   **Asymmetry**: favor uneven, \"scattered\" compositions over perfectly symmetric grids — content blocks can sit at a slight offset or rotation from a strict grid, echoing the confetti field.\n*   **Whitespace**: despite the color clash, generous cream negative space between elements is essential — it's what keeps the style feeling playful rather than cluttered.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Confetti shape field** as background decoration in every major section.\n*   **Thick black outlines** on every colored shape, always.\n*   **Multi-color simultaneous palette** (3+ accents active per screen).\n*   **Squiggle underlines** replacing straight lines for emphasis/links.\n*   **Playful hover rotation** on buttons and cards.\n*   **Multi-color headline treatment** (word-by-word color changes).\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: bouncy and cheerful — `duration-200` to `duration-300` with a slight overshoot easing (`cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1)`) on hover rotations.\n*   **Ambient motion**: confetti shapes can have very slow, subtle independent drift/rotation to feel \"alive,\" never fast or distracting.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: simple, rounded-stroke line icons (2px), often housed in an outlined circular chip filled with one accent color.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: because the palette is loud and multi-hue, verify every text-on-accent-fill combination individually against WCAG AA — some pastel-leaning accents (yellow especially) need dark text, not white.\n*   **Motion**: respect `prefers-reduced-motion` for confetti drift and hover rotation.\n*   **Touch Targets**: maintain 44px minimum despite playful shapes — decoration should never shrink a tap target.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: reduce confetti shape count (4-6 instead of 6-12) to avoid visual clutter on small screens; keep at least one accent color and the black-outline rule intact on every card/button.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Marketing / e-commerce sites**: full confetti hero, multi-color product cards, playful hover rotations.\n*   **SaaS dashboards / internal tools**: use the outline + single-accent-per-element rule for cards and buttons; keep confetti decoration to empty states, onboarding, and marketing chrome — dense data views should drop the scattered shapes but keep the friendly rounded, outlined component language.\n*   **Kids' / education / creative products**: this style's most natural home — full expression encouraged.\n*   **Mobile apps**: simplified confetti in onboarding/splash screens, outlined pill buttons throughout.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill memphis-postmodern-ui-light   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
